-- title:
--   Principal kernel of a retraction of a smooth relative curve stalk
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative local ring, let $X$ be a scheme and let $c \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ be a morphism which is smooth of relative dimension $1$, and let $x$ be a point of $X$. Write $u \colon A \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ for the composite of the inverse of the canonical isomorphism $A \cong \Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} A, \mathcal{O})$, the map on global sections induced by $c$, and the germ map $\Gamma(X,\mathcal{O}_X) \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ at $x$; the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,x} =$ `X.presheaf.stalk x` is regarded as an $A$-algebra through $u$. Let $\pi \colon \mathcal{O}_{X,x} \to A$ be any homomorphism of $A$-algebras for this structure. Then there exists $t \in \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ such that the kernel of the underlying ring homomorphism of $\pi$ is the principal ideal $(t)$, and such that the maximal ideal of the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ equals the sum of $(t)$ with the ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ generated by the image $u(\mathfrak{m}_A)$ of the maximal ideal of $A$. Thus $t$ both generates the kernel of the retraction and is a parameter along the fibre.
--
--   This is the local (retraction) form of the statement that a section of a smooth relative curve is cut out by a single function which is simultaneously a uniformiser on the fibre: the relative differentials being free of rank one at $x$, the kernel of an $A$-algebra retraction of the stalk is principal and generates the maximal ideal modulo $\mathfrak{m}_A$. It is phrased for a retraction given by algebra rather than by a scheme-theoretic section, and is used in the analysis of orders of vanishing at points of models of modular curves, in [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP.ord_placeOfPoint_eq_sum_ite_of_not_mem_ssPlacesQExp_of_mul_coeffMap_eq_coeffMap`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP.ord_placeOfPoint_eq_sum_ite_of_not_mem_ssPlacesQExp_of_mul_coeffMap_eq_coeffMap).
